\begin{abstract}
Zero-shot action recognition (ZSAR) requires transferring a visual-language model to action categories it was never trained on. CLIP aligns images and text well, but adapting it directly to video underperforms because its attention has no mechanism for temporal dynamics, and its class-level prompts carry little motion semantics. We present the Spatiotemporal Dynamic Duo (STDD), which turns CLIP into a spatiotemporal expert on both sides. On the vision side, Space-time Cross Attention (STCA) rewrites each ViT block's spatial attention as space--time cross attention through window shift masking, multi-scale channel mixing, spatial padding and residual shortcutting, adding no parameters and keeping the $O(TN^2)$ cost of spatial attention. On the text side, an Action Semantic Knowledge Graph (ASKG) built with an LLM separates each action into static (object) and dynamic (sub-action) concepts and generates spatial and temporal prompts. Training aligns frame features to prompts with a symmetric max-over-frames/prompts score, and distills from frozen CLIP. Trained on Kinetics-400, STDD reaches 93.4\% (EP1) and 88.6\% (EP2) on UCF101 with ViT-L/14, and 85.2\% on UCF101, 55.9\% on HMDB51 and 75.1\% on Kinetics-600 with ViT-B/16 under the EP3 protocol. The margins over the strongest prior methods are small, often within the reported standard deviations, and we discuss this in the experiments.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/1_teaser.png}
  \caption{Motivation: some novel actions are hard to separate from static appearance alone, and need motion cues.}
  \label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

Zero-shot action recognition asks a model to classify videos of actions that were absent from its training set, using only the semantics of the action names. Vision-language models such as CLIP \citep{radford2021learning} make this feasible for images, because a single embedding space links pictures to text. Video, however, is harder. Many novel actions cannot be told apart from a single frame, as with weightlifting variants that share a scene but differ in motion. A CLIP image encoder applied frame by frame does not model how appearance changes over time, and naive fine-tuning on video tends to underperform the frozen model on unseen classes.

Existing adaptations fall into three groups. Some add temporal modules, which increases parameters or attention cost \citep{bertasius2021space,arnab2021vivit,yang2023aim}. Some keep the image encoder and rely on class-level prompts such as ``a video of \{class\}'' \citep{wang2021actionclip,ni2022expanding}. Others enrich the text side with LLM-generated descriptions but leave the video encoder without temporal modeling \citep{chen2024ost,huang2024froster,jia2024generating}.

We take the view that CLIP should become a multi-modal spatiotemporal expert: the video stream should model cross-frame dynamics, and the text stream should describe both what the action looks like and how it unfolds. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
  \item A parameter-free Space-time Cross Attention that turns CLIP's spatial attention into space--time cross attention with the same asymptotic cost as spatial attention.
  \item An Action Semantic Knowledge Graph that disentangles static and dynamic semantics and produces spatial and temporal prompts for each action.
  \item A symmetric frame--prompt alignment objective, with distillation from frozen CLIP, that matches video frames to fine-grained prompts.
  \item Zero-shot results on UCF101, HMDB51 and Kinetics-600 under the EP1, EP2 and EP3 protocols that are the highest reported means in our comparison tables, with margins discussed against the reported variance.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\paragraph{CLIP for video.} CLIP-based video recognition has been adapted through prompt learning, adapters and temporal modules. ActionCLIP \citep{wang2021actionclip} recasts action recognition as video-text matching. X-CLIP \citep{ni2022expanding} adds cross-frame communication and video-specific prompts. Open-VCLIP \citep{weng2023open} and Open-VCLIP++ \citep{wu2024building} extend CLIP to an open vocabulary through weight interpolation and better training. Text4Vis \citep{wu2023revisiting} and BIKE \citep{wu2023bike} study how to adapt the image encoder for video. Our method differs in that it changes the attention itself rather than adding a module on top of it.

\paragraph{Temporal modeling without parameters.} Shift operations move channels between neighbouring frames at zero FLOP and parameter cost \citep{wu2018shift,lin2019tsm}. STDD builds on this idea inside the attention of a ViT block, combined with window shift masking.

\paragraph{Semantic augmentation.} Textual descriptions for actions have been produced by hand, from word embeddings, or with LLMs \citep{gao2019know,chen2021elaborative,kim2021daszl}. OST \citep{chen2024ost} uses spatiotemporal descriptors and FROSTER \citep{huang2024froster} uses action descriptions. Our ASKG adds explicit relations between objects and sub-actions.

\section{Method: Spatiotemporal Dynamic Duo}
\subsection{Overview}
STDD changes both encoders. The vision encoder is a CLIP ViT whose attention is replaced by STCA (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). The text encoder is the frozen CLIP text encoder, applied to prompts generated from the ASKG (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}). Only the vision encoder is updated during training.

