\subsection{Space-time Cross Attention}
Let a video have $T$ frames, each split into $N$ patches plus a per-frame [CLS] token. A standard ViT block computes $z'^{l}_t = \mathrm{MHSA}(\mathrm{LN}(z^{l-1}_t)) + z^{l-1}_t$ and $z^{l}_t = \mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{LN}(z'^{l}_t)) + \hat z^{l}_t$, where $\hat z^{l}_t$ is produced by four steps.

\paragraph{Window shift masking (WSM).} A spatial window of $w_1\times w_2$ (default $2\times 2$) is defined per frame, and a mask with ratio $r$ (default $0.5$) discards tokens. The mask shifts over time, so different positions survive in different frames. The masked tokens $\dot z^{l-1}_t$ contain $N' = rN$ tokens per frame and interleave spatial positions across time.

\paragraph{Multi-scale channel mixing (MCM).} Temporal dynamics are injected by shifting channels from frames at temporal offset $\delta$. With $d_\delta = \gamma D$, the separate mixing for offset $\delta$ takes the first $d_\delta$ channels from frame $t-\delta$, the next $d_\delta$ from frame $t+\delta$, and the rest from frame $t$. The continual variant mixes denser context, taking $d_\delta/\delta$ channels from each of $t\pm 1,\dots,t\pm\delta$. MHSA is applied to the mixed tokens, and the outputs are averaged over scales $\delta\in\{\pm 1,\pm 2\}$ (default).

\paragraph{Spatial padding and shortcutting.} Because WSM keeps $N'$ tokens, the result is padded back to $N$: masked positions take the standard-path output $z'^{l}_t$ and unmasked positions take the mixed output. The padded tensor $\hat z^{l}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\times D}$ restores the spatial layout, and the residual connection fuses the dynamic signal.

\paragraph{Complexity.} STCA costs $O(TN^2 + S\,T\,(N')^2)$, which is $O(TN^2)$ because $N' = rN$ and $S$, $r$ are constants. Factorised space-time attention in AIM costs $O(TN^2 + T^2N)$ and full space--time attention $O(T^2N^2)$ \citep{yang2023aim}.

\subsection{Spatiotemporal Text Augmentation}
The ASKG is built by prompting an LLM (GPT-3.5 in our setup) for each action. Its nodes are the action, related objects and sub-actions, and its edges are predicates between entities, such as ``person--uses--rope'' or ``person--descends--vertical surface'' for abseiling. For each category we request between 5 and 10 objects or sub-actions and concise predicates between entity pairs. Spatial prompts $C^{s}$ are sentences built from object--relation triples and describe static appearance and context. Temporal prompts $C^{t}$ are sentences built from sub-action--relation triples and describe motion and progression. The prompt set is $C^{st} = C^{s} \cup C^{t}$, and each prompt is encoded by the frozen CLIP text encoder.

\subsection{Training and Inference}
Frame-level [CLS] features $z_{n,t}$ are compared with the prompt features $c^{st}_{k,j}$ through two scores. The frame-to-prompt score is $S^{v2t}_{n,k} = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t} \max_{j} z_{n,t}^{\top} c^{st}_{k,j}$, and the prompt-to-frame score is $S^{t2v}_{n,k} = \frac{1}{N^{st}}\sum_{j} \max_{t} z_{n,t}^{\top} c^{st}_{k,j}$. The final score $S_{n,k} = (S^{v2t}_{n,k} + S^{t2v}_{n,k})/2$ is optimised with cross-entropy over the seen classes. A feature-level distillation term from the frozen CLIP video features preserves generalisation. At test time, each video is scored with 3 temporal and 1 spatial view, and predictions are averaged.

\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Setup}
Models are trained on Kinetics-400 with CLIP ViT-B/16 and ViT-L/14 backbones, using 8-frame clips. STCA defaults are a $2\times2$ window, mask ratio $r=0.5$ and temporal scales $\{\pm1,\pm2\}$. We evaluate zero-shot on UCF101 and HMDB51 under EP1, EP2 and EP3, and on Kinetics-600 (K600). Baselines are those listed in the tables. All numbers are top-1 accuracy (\%); values with $\pm$ are mean and standard deviation over the reported runs. Numbers are taken from the supplied tables and are not re-run here.

\subsection{Comparison under EP1 and EP2}
Table~\ref{ep1&2} compares the two backbones with prior methods. With ViT-L/14, STDD reaches 93.4 and 88.6 on UCF101 (EP1, EP2) and 68.7 and 58.7 on HMDB51, the highest means in the table. Against Open-VCLIP with ViT-L/14, the gains are 0.3 and 0.7 on UCF101 and 0.2 and 0.4 on HMDB51. With ViT-B/16, STDD reaches 90.3 and 85.3 on UCF101 and 64.7 and 54.7 on HMDB51. The EP2 gains over the ViT-B/16 row for Text4Vis$^{*}$ (re-evaluated by its authors, 83.5 and 53.2) are 1.8 and 1.5 points. That row is the one the supplied table reports for ViT-B/16; no Open-VCLIP ViT-B/16 row is given, so we do not attribute these numbers to Open-VCLIP. The EP1 gains are within the reported standard deviations.

