\section{Limitations}
The ASKG is generated by an LLM, which can introduce noise or bias; automatic quality control or human refinement could reduce this. The hyperparameters, namely the mask ratio, window size, temporal scales and $\gamma$, may need tuning across datasets. The temporal scales are fixed, and adaptive or content-aware scale selection could improve robustness. The method is evaluated on short clips, and long-form videos or multi-person interactions may need hierarchical temporal modelling. Finally, several margins over the strongest baselines are within the reported standard deviations, so the improvements should be read as competitive, not as established gains in every setting. Training details beyond those given in the method, such as the distillation weight, optimiser and schedule, are not in the source material and are not reported here.

\section{Conclusion}
STDD adapts CLIP to zero-shot action recognition on both sides of the model. Space-time Cross Attention adds temporal interaction to the vision encoder without parameters or extra asymptotic cost, and the ASKG supplies static and dynamic prompts that the frame--prompt alignment uses. The method reaches the highest reported means in our comparisons on UCF101, HMDB51 and Kinetics-600, with gains that are modest and in several cases within the reported variance.

